(Originally a combination of {#na#} and {#nu#}, now
 used as a separate word) A particle implying:--
.²1 Inquiry or interrogation; {#nanu samAptakftyo gOtamaH#} <ls>M. 4</ls>.
.²2 Surely, certainly, indeed, is it not indeed (with an interrogative
 force); {#yadA'meDAvinI SizyopadeSaM malinayati tadAcAryasya dozo nanu#} <ls>M. 1</ls>.
.²3 Of course, indeed, certainly ({#avaDAraRam#}); {#upapannaM nanu SivaM#}
 {#saptasvaNgezu#} <ls>R. 1. 60</ls>; {#trilokanATena sadA maKadvizastvayA niyamyA#}
 {#nanu divyacakzuzA#} <ls n="R.">3. 45</ls>.
.²4 It is used as a vocative particle meaning ‘O’, ‘Oh’; {#nanu mAnava#}
 <ls>Dk.</ls>; {#nanu mUrKAH paWitameva yuzmABistatkARqe#} <ls>U. 4</ls>.
.²5 It is used in propitiatory expressions in the sense of ‘pray’, ‘be
 pleased’; {#nanu mAM prApaya patyurantikam#} <ls>Ku. 4. 32</ls>.
.²6 It is sometimes used as a corrective word like the <lang>English</lang> ‘why’ or
 ‘I say’; {#nanu pade parivftya BaRa#} <ls>Mk. 5</ls>; {#nanu BaRAmi cintita upAya#}
 {#iti#} <ls>V. 2</ls>; {#nanu BavAnagrato me vartate#} <ls>Ś. 2</ls>; {#nanu vicinotu BavAn#} <ls>V. 2</ls>.
.²7 In argumentative discussions {#nanu#} is frequently used to head an
 objection or advance a contrary proposition (generally followed by {#ucyate#});
 {#nanvacetanAnyeva vfScikAdiSarIrARi acetanAnAM ca gomayAdInAM kAryARIti#}
 {#ucyate#} <ls>Ś. B.</ls>
